They carry less weight than the measured ones.


What a result covers

Not every field came from the implementation. Three of the five expose
no entry point that yields the parsed announce fields; their validator
returns a boolean and nothing else. For those the harness follows the
implementation's own code step by step, using its constants and its
primitives, and marks the place. Such fields test the transcription as
much as the implementation.

    implementation    derivations   announce fields   verdicts
    ------------------------------------------------------------
    rsReticulum       called        called            called
    Reticulum-rs      called        transcribed       called
    go-reticulum      called        transcribed       called
    rns.js            called        transcribed       called
    microReticulum    called        transcribed       called

Derivations are the identity hash, the name hash, the destination hash,
Ed25519 verification, and for the encrypted vectors the key derivation
and the decryption. Verdicts are signature_valid, hmac_valid, the
plaintext, and where the implementation offers one, its own accept or
reject. All findings below rest on called code or on source, not on
transcription.

Two of the harnesses reach past the implementation for the X25519
agreement alone, because go-reticulum and Reticulum-rs keep the private
key unexported. Both use their language's standard curve, which is what
those implementations use internally.

All six agree on the identity hash, the destination hash for names with
at least one aspect, the announce payload layout, the assembly of
signed data including the position of app data, and Ed25519
verification.

Four of the five decrypt a packet from the reference correctly. The
encrypted vectors turned up one implementation that cannot decrypt any
of them, and one whose ratchets do not work, both for reasons the
announce vectors could not have shown.


Finding 1: the hop limit is not applied at parse time

Affects every implementation tested. Vector test/announce/hop-limit.

python-rns rejects a packet whose hop count is at or above
PATHFINDER_M, that is 128, inside Packet.unpack, before any other
field is examined (RNS/Packet.py:247). None of the implementations do
this. All decode the packet fully and report it as a valid announce.

Where a limit exists at all, it sits further in and reads differently:

    go-reticulum      transport.go:4400   hops >= 128 + 1
    rsReticulum       ingress.rs:372      hops >= 128 - 1, announces only
    sgiath            transport.ex:36     routing_max_hops, source only
    rns.js            none found
    Reticulum-rs      none found
    microReticulum    none checked at parse

go-reticulum's threshold is one higher than the reference. A packet at
exactly 128 hops is dropped by python-rns and forwarded by
go-reticulum.

This is the one finding shared by every implementation, which suggests
the rule is not obvious from reading the reference. It is now
doc/packet, section Hops.


Finding 2: ratchet announces are rejected

Affects Reticulum-rs. Vectors test/announce/ratchet and test/announce/ratchet-and-app-data.

DestinationAnnounce::validate (destination.rs:115) never reads the
context flag and never parses a ratchet. The 32 ratchet bytes are read
as the first half of the signature, so every announce carrying a
ratchet fails signature verification and is dropped.

This is the trap described in doc/announce, section Ratchet. It is the
most consequential finding here: ratchets are enabled per destination
in current python-rns, and an affected node silently loses those
announces rather than reporting an error.


Finding 3: an app name with no aspects gets a trailing dot

Affects Reticulum-rs and microReticulum. Vector test/destination/app-name-only.

The reference joins app name and aspects with a dot between each pair,
so a destination with no aspects hashes the app name alone. Both
implementations append a separator unconditionally:

    Reticulum-rs      destination.rs:70    app_name || "." || aspects
    microReticulum    Destination.cpp:180  if (aspects != nullptr)

microReticulum's guard tests for a null pointer, but the empty-aspect
call path passes "" rather than nullptr (Destination.cpp:164), so the
dot is appended.

    reference       example_utilities     8f28004423519cc633ca
    both            example_utilities.    ada8ea63426e6118c006

Every destination whose name is an app name alone is unreachable
between these implementations and the reference.


Finding 4: non-canonical X25519 scalars

Affects rns.js and microReticulum. Vectors test/keyset/unclamped and test/keyset/clamped.

The reference stores the X25519 private scalar exactly as supplied and
clamps only when the scalar is used, so an unclamped scalar
round-trips unchanged and yields the same public key as its clamped
form. See doc/identity.

    rns.js            rejects the key outright. @noble/curves clamps
                      first and then requires the result to be below
                      2^255 - 19, which excludes two of the 2^251
                      reachable clamped values.

    microReticulum    accepts the key and derives a different public
                      key, and therefore a different identity hash. It
                      does not clamp before the scalar multiplication.

rns.js's case is an edge case: all five upstream keys are already
clamped, and both python-rns backends clamp when generating an identity
key, so a stored identity never reaches that path.

microReticulum's is not, and finding 6 below shows why. It fails
silently with a wrong public key rather than refusing the input, and
ratchet keys are not clamped when generated.


sgiath/reticulum, from source only

Read against the same facts. Nothing contradicting the corpus was
found:

    identity hash     sha256(enc_pub || sig_pub)[:16]   identity.ex:14
    name hash         join(".") over app name and aspects, no spurious
                      separator                          destination.ex:124
    announce layout   context flag drives the ratchet field
                                                         announce.ex:100
    signed data       destination hash first, app data last
                                                         announce.ex:158
    binding check     name hash and identity hash, as the reference
                                                         announce.ex:148

Its flag decoding splits the byte differently, reading bits 5 and 4 as
one two-bit field and recovering the context flag and transport type
from it (packet.ex:66, packet.ex:151). That is equivalent to the
reference for every value the corpus contains.

It reads bit 7 as an IFAC flag where python-rns 1.4.2 treats that bit
as reserved. No vector sets bit 7, so this was not exercised.

These observations are worth what source reading is worth. Running the
corpus against it would settle them.


Finding 5: rns.js derives half the key material

Affects rns.js. All five test/encrypted/ vectors.

    src/identity.js:237     Cryptography.hkdf(32, sharedKey, this.hash)
    RNS/Identity.py:823     length=Identity.DERIVED_KEY_LENGTH, that is 64

A 64-byte derived key selects AES-256-CBC and splits into two 32-byte
halves; a 32-byte key selects AES-128-CBC and splits into two 16-byte
halves. rns.js takes the second path throughout, down to
src/fernet.js:76, which names aes-128-cbc outright.

The HKDF output agrees as far as it goes: rns.js's signing key is the
first half of the reference's signing key. Only the length differs.

    reference   signing 61d1de0d...639e6aff  encryption a00a15a6...3bdcd28c
    rns.js      signing 61d1de0d73097c427032bac52d5376b0
                encryption 46b9856a53d1e8841cc6f8c9639e6aff

The consequence is total for single-destination traffic: rns.js cannot
decrypt a packet from python-rns 1.4.2, and python-rns cannot decrypt
one from rns.js. The HMAC fails before decryption is even attempted.

python-rns keeps DERIVED_KEY_LENGTH_LEGACY = 32 at RNS/Identity.py:91,
so 32 was once right. This is version skew rather than a misreading,
and it is what a corpus pinned to a version is for.


Finding 6: microReticulum does not clamp, and that breaks ratchets

Affects microReticulum. Vector test/encrypted/ratchet, and the same
root cause as finding 4.

Finding 4 recorded microReticulum deriving a different public key from
a non-canonical X25519 scalar, and called it an edge case because every
key the reference generates is already clamped. Ratchet keys are not.
RNS/Identity.py:404 generates one as 32 raw random bytes, and a random
scalar is already in clamped form only once in 32 tries.

    ratchet private     267c9c1b...00d48784   as generated, non-canonical
    clamped form        207c9c1b...00d48744
    reference public    c82c57db...1a52fe32   from the clamped scalar
    microReticulum      fa7c42ed...           from the raw scalar

The shared secret differs, the HMAC fails, and the packet is dropped.
microReticulum decrypts the three identity-key vectors correctly, so
the defect is invisible until a ratchet is used.


Finding 7: microReticulum does not build with GCC 16

src/microReticulum/Utilities/Memory.h uses uint8_t and uint32_t without
including <cstdint>, which older libstdc++ headers supplied
transitively. Building the library needs -include cstdint. Not a wire
format matter, but it stands between the corpus and anyone who wants to
reproduce the microReticulum row.


Reproducing

    ENCODE=no DUMP=conformance/<impl>/<binary> cmd/check

ENCODE=no because no harness implements the -e direction; it belongs to
the corpus, not to the wire format. See conformance/README for where to
put each implementation and how to build its harness. The diff output
of cmd/check names the exact vector and field behind every claim above.
